
When a real atom is transformed into a dummy atom with full flexibility, 
the stretch-bend file (oplsaa.sb) must be modified to contain entries for
the bonds and angles to the dummy atoms.  Bond lengths to dummy atoms
should have an equilibrium value shorter
than the hard-shell van der Waals radius of the connecting atom to 
prevent convergence problems owing to cavity formation.  Typically a 
value of 0.4 Angstroms is sufficient.  At least one angle to each dummy
atom is required to avert linear angles that can cause some numerical instability.

To improve convergence, however, the MAXIMUM number of angles to each
dummy atom should be assigned non-zero force constants, provided that
they do not spuriously constrain real atoms.  The latter situation
occurs if a dummy atom has more than two 1,3 partners
that are real atoms, and non-zero force constants are assigned 
to at least three of the angles between them.  This essentially increases 
the force constants between the angles involving only the real atoms, 
and can potentially alter the equilibrium value
of the angle between the real atoms.  In general, the equilibrium values
and motions of real atoms SHOULD NOT be affected by the addition of dummy
atoms in the z-matrix.  To achieve the best convergence without     
nonphysically coupling real atoms, all angles that are being transformed
from Y-X-Z -> DM-X-DM should have unchanging force constants, and two or
less angles being transformed from Y-X-Z -> DM-X-Z should have unchanging  
force constants (where X, Y, and Z are real atoms).  Remaining DM-X-Z
angles should have zero force constants.

As an example, an ethane to methane mutation should have DM-HC-CT and  
DM-HC-DM defined with non-zero force constants equivalent to their  
HC-CT-CT and HC-CT-HC counterparts (that is, all of the angles to dummy
atoms  are defined).  However, an aniline to toluene mutation should have
DM-N-H defined with a force constants equal to HC-CT-HC; DM-N-CA should
have a k(theta)=0.

Note: if the entries for the dummy atom are not made in the oplsaa.sb
file, the force constant and zero value are taken to be the same as
when the atom was not a dummy. E.g., if CT-OH-HO is being perturbed
to CT-HC-DM, and an entry is not made for HC-DM in the oplsaa.sb file,
it is taken to be the same as for OH-HO.

